And my, my mind was red, except the red blazer didn't quite match the red pants.
而我的……我的夹克是红色的，只不过红色西装外套跟红色裤子不太搭。

00:18:00.049 --> 00:18:01.049
Look at it.
你看。

00:18:01.049 --> 00:18:02.049
It's two different rads.
这是两种不同的红。

00:18:02.049 --> 00:18:03.049
Like Barney would have been like, what in the world?
就像巴尼会说：“这到底是什么鬼？”

00:18:03.049 --> 00:18:04.049
So we gave away two red suits.
所以我们送了两套红西装。

00:18:04.049 --> 00:18:05.049
Yeah.
对。

00:18:05.049 --> 00:18:06.049
Two red suits.
两套红西装。

00:18:06.049 --> 00:18:09.970
They were somewhat similar and I was like, sold.
它们有点相似，然后我就想，买了。

00:18:09.970 --> 00:18:13.009
I've got three dollars and I'm looking to spend it.
我身上有三块钱，正想把它花掉。

00:18:13.009 --> 00:18:14.009
Okay.
好的。

00:18:14.009 --> 00:18:24.690
So you guys, you're senior year, you, you create enough comedy that you have this comedy packet enough to write, send it out to agents and staffing.
所以你们俩，在大四的时候，你们创作了足够的喜剧内容，弄出了这样一个喜剧包，足以把它寄给经纪人和剧组人员。

00:18:24.690 --> 00:18:41.450
This is a good chance to give a shout out to somebody who, so, you remember those people and you've had them too, Josh, where the person that kind of changed your life, right, Carter, this executive in MTV, we were there getting him coffee and getting sushi and making photo copies and we were writing our little comedy ideas.
这是一个好机会，可以向某个人表达谢意，所以，你还记得那些人，你也有过这样的人，乔许，就是那种改变了你人生轨迹的人，对吧，卡特，MTV的这位高管，我们在那里给他买咖啡、买寿司、复印文件，同时写着我们的小喜剧创意。

00:18:41.450 --> 00:18:45.809
We were just like, we had been in a band and we started writing comedy together because we clicked so much.
我们当时就觉得……我们曾在一个乐队里，然后我们开始一起写喜剧，因为我们太默契了。

00:18:45.809 --> 00:18:47.849
We started writing our own ideas and submitting it to this executive.
我们开始写自己的创意，并把它提交给这位高管。

00:18:47.849 --> 00:19:03.089
this guy, Jeremiah Boschang, who had been executive at NBC, he had worked on Seinfeld and now he's in MTV and he saw our little ideas and thought there were clever and interesting enough that he submitted, he, I think, submitted them for us Carter and maybe gave us permission to connect it.
这个人，耶利米·博尚，他曾是NBC的高管，他曾在《宋飞正传》工作过，现在他在MTV，他看到了我们的小创意，觉得它们足够聪明和有趣，于是他提交了……他，我猜，为我们提交了它们，卡特，也许还允许我们建立联系。

00:19:03.089 --> 00:19:11.588
No, he gave us the, the, the, it was, I mean, this is how, I, like, people, I'm, I'm sure people ask you, like, how, how do you get your start?
不，他给了我们……这就是……我的意思是，这就是，就像，人们，我确信人们会问你，比如，你是怎么入行的？

00:19:11.588 --> 00:19:15.569
And it's like, our start is so not connected with anything in 2025.
然后就会发现，我们的入行经历跟2025年的任何东西都毫无关系。

00:19:15.569 --> 00:19:17.809
Like it's sort of like old school.
就像它有点像老派风格。

00:19:17.809 --> 00:19:25.450
He literally was like send a packet of material with a cover letter that you print out and put in the mail.
他字面上的意思是，打印出一包材料，附上一封求职信，然后寄到邮局。

00:19:25.450 --> 00:19:29.089
And in the cover letter, mention my name in the first sentence.
在求职信的第一句话里，提到我的名字。

00:19:29.089 --> 00:19:42.569
And that was like all the, like that was the little like just tights, gauss number thin filament of like connection to like if he doesn't read that first sentence then it goes on some, it goes in the fire and then that's it.
这就成了全部的……就像那是唯一的一点，就像紧身裤、高斯数字、薄薄的细丝般的联系，如果他没有读到第一句话，那么它就会被转给某人，就会付之一炬，然后就结束了。

00:19:42.569 --> 00:19:44.170
It wasn't really email, right?
那时候其实还没有电子邮件，对吧？

00:19:44.170 --> 00:19:48.730
There was like, yeah, there was, we were still in the era where you went to the Science Center in college to send an email.
那时候就像是，对，我们仍然处于那种你必须去大学的科学中心才能发送电子邮件的时代。

00:19:48.730 --> 00:19:51.730
I've gotta go to the Science Center to send out this message.
我得去科学中心把这条信息发出去。

00:19:51.730 --> 00:19:53.089
I've been trying to do snow.
我一直在试图做一些新鲜事。

00:19:53.089 --> 00:19:57.890
We found a thousand years old in this entire, the theme of this whole episode is we're so fucking old.
我们发现了一千年历史的东西，整整……这一整集的主题就是我们特么的太老了。

00:19:57.890 --> 00:19:59.930
That's the theme of this entire thing so far.
到目前为止，这就是整个节目的主题。

00:19:59.930 --> 00:20:06.170
But somehow that worked out and by that guy believing in us, it got to an agent, an agency, who was one of the partners there.
但不知何故，这居然成功了，因为那个人对我们的信任，它交到了一位经纪人、一家经纪公司手中，那是那里的合伙人之一。

00:20:06.170 --> 00:20:10.009
This guy, Elliot Webb, was Broder, Kirland Webb, I think.
这个人，埃利奥特·韦伯，我记得是布罗德·柯兰·韦伯公司的人。

00:20:10.009 --> 00:20:16.210
And he was like, I'm a partner, I'm too big for this thing, but they seem clever and they seem, I like this guy Jeremiah.
他当时说：“我是合伙人，这种事对我来说太大材小用了，但他们看起来很聪明，而且我觉得这个叫耶利米的人不错。”

00:20:16.210 --> 00:20:22.009
So he gave it to literally the guy who became our agent and his agent to this day, Matt Rice.
所以他把它直接交给了后来成为我们经纪人的人，而且至今仍是他的经纪人，马特·赖斯。

00:20:22.009 --> 00:20:28.890
So again, our story is useless to anyone because number one, it sounds ancient and also number two, it's, it's impossible.
所以再次说明，我们的故事对任何人来说都没用，因为第一，它听起来像远古时代的事，第二，它……它是不可能的。

00:20:28.890 --> 00:20:30.289
That should not have worked.
那本不该成功的。

00:20:30.289 --> 00:20:31.490
We sent out one thing.
我们只寄出了一份东西。

00:20:31.490 --> 00:20:35.089
The guy actually gave it to a junior agent who became roasted to ranks.
那个人居然把它转给了一个初级经纪人，而他后来成了大腕。

00:20:35.089 --> 00:20:37.490
Matt Rice is like a big powerful agent and well deserved with these.
马特·赖斯就像一个非常有权势的大经纪人，而且实至名归。

00:20:37.490 --> 00:20:38.490
So he's amazing.
所以他太棒了。

00:20:38.490 --> 00:20:39.490
Yeah, he just started.
对，他当时刚起步。

00:20:39.490 --> 00:20:40.490
He just started.
他才刚开始干。

00:20:40.490 --> 00:20:41.450
at this point, yeah, I think.
在这个时间点，对，我猜。

00:20:41.450 --> 00:20:42.450
Yeah, he was like five.
对，他当时大概五岁吧。

00:20:42.450 --> 00:20:45.970
Was he, were you guys like kind of his first clients?
是吗，你们俩算是他的第一批客户吗？

00:20:45.970 --> 00:20:47.569
No, but you know who was?
不，但你知道谁才是吗？

00:20:47.569 --> 00:20:50.809
Will Forte and Chris Miller and Phil Lord were among his first clients.
威尔·法瑞尔、克里斯·米勒和菲尔·洛德是他的第一批客户。

00:20:50.809 --> 00:20:53.289
They was like, we were a few in past them.
就像我们是在他们之后的一小批。

00:20:53.289 --> 00:20:54.849
So that's how good this guy is.
所以这个人就是这么厉害。

00:20:54.849 --> 00:21:01.009
And like being a good agent really, there really is a like instinctual super power, spidey sense thing that happens when he has it.
而且作为一个优秀的经纪人，确实有一种直觉般的超能力，一种蜘蛛感应，当他具备这种能力时就会显现出来。

00:21:01.009 --> 00:21:08.130
And we were, so we were in a great company right away and Matt Rice helped us get to who's our manager at the time, David Minor.
所以我们，我们一上来就加入了一家很棒的公司，马特·赖斯帮我们找到了我们当时的经纪人，大卫·迈纳。

00:21:08.130 --> 00:21:10.849
all kind of toward the end and coming out of college.
这些都发生在大学快结束和毕业的时候。

00:21:10.849 --> 00:21:15.349
And that's somehow led to us getting our Letterman material.
而这不知何故让我们获得了为莱特曼（Letterman）供稿的机会。

00:21:15.349 --> 00:21:20.450
We'd written some Conan in material, we'd written a sign fell, we'd written SNL ideas, but Letterman was hiring.
我们写过一些柯南（Conan）的段子，我们写过《宋飞正传》，我们写过《周六夜现场》（SNL）的创意，但当时莱特曼正在招人。

00:21:20.450 --> 00:21:23.289
Like Conan was like, we were obsessed with our dream with Conan.
就像柯南……我们对柯南有着痴迷的梦想。

00:21:23.289 --> 00:21:25.529
That was the biggest part of it also was like, we want to live in New York.
这也是其中最大的一部分，就像，我们想住在纽约。

00:21:25.529 --> 00:21:28.049
Neither of us had any interest in going to LA.
我们俩对去洛杉矶都毫无兴趣。

00:21:28.049 --> 00:21:35.619
And so that really at the time, it was SNL, Conan, Letterman, Daily Show.
所以当时真正的选择其实是《周六夜现场》、柯南、莱特曼、《每日秀》。

00:21:35.619 --> 00:21:39.299
show, the Daily Show, I think maybe the Craig Killborne Daily Show may have existed.
节目，《每日秀》，我猜当时克雷格·基尔本（Craig Kilborn）的《每日秀》可能已经开播了。

00:21:39.299 --> 00:21:42.859
But I don't think we were, we were, yeah, we weren't trying that.
但我觉得我们当时没……我们没去尝试那个。

00:21:42.859 --> 00:21:43.359
But yeah.
不过是的。

00:21:43.359 --> 00:21:47.794
But it was like there's four jobs for comedy writers in, in New York in, in 1997.
但这就好像在1997年的纽约，喜剧编剧只有四个工作机会。

00:21:47.794 --> 00:21:49.547
And we were obsessed with Conan.
而我们对柯南痴迷不已。

00:21:49.539 --> 00:21:53.806
It was like the year of like, where like Robert Smigel was running it.
那正是罗伯特·斯米格尔（Robert Smigel）掌权的那一年。

00:21:53.806 --> 00:21:55.940
And it was just like going bananas.
情况简直疯狂。

00:21:55.940 --> 00:22:02.196
And just like every, like, it was, it was definitely like, if I, if I'm gonna do anything for living.
就像每个人都……这绝对是那种“如果我要以这个为生”的想法。

00:22:02.196 --> 00:22:04.860
I want to, I want to write this dumb stuff.
我想，我想写这些蠢东西。

00:22:04.860 --> 00:22:08.144
It was definitely the weirdest thing happening on TV.
这绝对是当时电视上发生的最诡异的事情。

00:22:08.144 --> 00:22:08.644
It was.
确实是的。

00:22:08.644 --> 00:22:09.144
It was.
确实是的。

00:22:09.144 --> 00:22:10.692
It was like very strange.
它非常奇怪。

00:22:10.692 --> 00:22:11.374
Like, yeah.
就像，是的。

00:22:11.374 --> 00:22:18.312
Can I just push back on, on, on, on your, on your path to success a little bit as a thing that isn't repeatable?
我能对你们的成功之路……对你们那种不可复制的成功之路，稍微提出一点异议吗？

00:22:19.539 --> 00:22:21.385
Another way I, I think I'll look at it.
我换个角度来看待它。

00:22:21.385 --> 00:22:22.663
Greg is Letterman's nephew.
格雷格是莱特曼的侄子。

00:22:22.663 --> 00:22:23.163
Okay.
好吧。

00:22:26.900 --> 00:22:29.645
Isn't exactly the way that you make it.
这并不完全是你成功的方式。

00:22:29.645 --> 00:22:42.740
You do the best that you can up until this one magical line, and you have to do the absolute best you can be incredibly talented, put in a huge amount of work, and that's all you can do.
你在划定的那条神奇的线之前，已经尽了最大努力，你必须做到最好，极具天赋，付出大量的心血，这也就是你能做的全部了。

00:22:42.740 --> 00:22:53.299
And then on the other side of the line is a whole series of luck, always, but that luck is because you've you've walked up to the door with the right materials and the right person open it.
而在这条线的另一边，总是充斥着各种运气，但这种运气是因为你带着正确的素材走到了门前，而合适的人打开了门。

00:22:53.299 --> 00:22:56.220
That's exactly the way it works, but we did work really hard.
事情确实是这样的，但我们确实非常努力。

00:22:56.220 --> 00:23:04.472
I'll give us that because Carter and I were that whole summer, we were recording music my mom's basement while also writing like three portfolios, right?
这点我承认，因为那整个夏天，我和卡特都在我妈妈的地下室里录制音乐，同时还要写大约三份作品集，对吧？

00:23:04.472 --> 00:23:09.002
Like Conan, like Letterman, and like Letterman was like the least fleshed out on it.
比如柯南秀，比如大卫深夜秀，而且大卫深夜秀的构思其实是最不成熟的。

00:23:09.002 --> 00:23:11.430
Well, there's just, it was the most exciting.
好吧，只是，那是让人最兴奋的。

00:23:11.430 --> 00:23:18.172
I mean, we spent the whole year working on our Conan packet because it was like, it's got to be Conan, this Conan or nothing.
我的意思是，我们花了一整年的时间来写柯南的申请材料，因为当时觉得，必须是柯南，要么是柯南，要么什么都没有。

00:23:18.172 --> 00:23:18.672
Yeah.
是的。

00:23:18.672 --> 00:23:19.750
You loved Letterman.
你以前很喜欢大卫·莱特曼。

00:23:19.750 --> 00:23:21.746
I had not watched a lot of Letterman.
我其实没怎么看过大卫·莱特曼的节目。

00:23:21.746 --> 00:23:24.443
I was like, I was not a Letterman guy necessarily.
我当时并不是那种非得看莱特曼的人。

00:23:24.443 --> 00:23:31.670
But then like David Minor, who who maddened hooked us up with who gets, you know, he's, we have him to thank for our career, for sure.
但后来像大卫·迈纳这样的人，他把我们引荐给别人，你知道的，我们的职业生涯绝对要感谢他。

00:23:31.670 --> 00:23:36.740
David Miner was like, he, uh, he basically gave us the news, like they're not hiring at Conan.
大卫·迈纳就像是，他基本上告诉了我们这个消息：柯南那边不招人。

00:23:36.740 --> 00:23:41.594
Everyone loves it there, obviously, because why would you ever quit this amazing fun show?
那里的每个人显然都热爱这份工作，废话，谁会愿意辞掉这么棒、这么有趣的节目呢？

00:23:41.594 --> 00:23:46.772
Uh, but two writers had just left Letterman and they were hiring and they're looking for people.
呃，但是莱特曼刚刚走了两个编剧，他们正在招人，在物色合适的人选。

00:23:46.772 --> 00:23:52.166
And it was, this is like on a Friday and it was like, can you get them a Letterman packet by Monday?
而且当时是周五，对方问：你们能在周一前交出一份莱特曼的申请材料吗？

00:23:52.166 --> 00:23:57.290
And we had a little bit of a start on it, but it was like maybe 20, 30% we just stayed up here.
我们其实已经开了个头，但大概只完成了百分之二十或三十，我们只能熬夜赶工。

00:23:57.059 --> 00:24:02.579
Each show needed a different packet, like tonally, because they were different.
每个节目都需要不同的材料，因为基调不同，它们的风格是不一样的。

00:24:02.579 --> 00:24:04.859
So a Conan has to be different than a letterman packet.
所以柯南的材料必须和莱特曼的不同。

00:24:04.859 --> 00:24:05.700
Especially a letterman.
尤其是莱特曼的节目。

00:24:05.700 --> 00:24:09.339
Because letterman is so like about his voice and about his like specific thing that he does.
因为莱特曼非常注重他个人的声音，注重他那种独特的风格。

00:24:09.339 --> 00:24:14.900
You had 72 hours to write an enormous amount of comedic material in the voice and style of David Letterman.
你们在72小时内，以大卫·莱特曼的嗓音和风格，写出了海量的喜剧段子。

00:24:14.900 --> 00:24:17.259
While living at my mother's house in Long Island.
当时还住在长岛我母亲的家里。

00:24:17.259 --> 00:24:19.220
Yeah, and but it's funny.
是的，不过这挺搞笑的。

00:24:19.220 --> 00:24:20.619
I've been graduated.
我已经毕业了。

00:24:20.619 --> 00:24:29.019
Yeah, we were like, yeah, we would like go to a diner and like, like, thank, you know, and by the way, Jane Thomas, I owe her my life as well, because she'd like put me up for that summer.
对，我们当时就像是，会去一家餐馆，然后，你知道的，顺便说一句，简·托马斯，我也欠她一条命，因为她那年夏天收留了我。

00:24:29.019 --> 00:24:30.859
Like I was like living in Craig's house.
就像我当时住在克雷格家里一样。

00:24:30.859 --> 00:24:32.180
How can I met my mother?
我怎么遇到我母亲的？

00:24:32.180 --> 00:24:44.259
But I just remember it's like the, there was a moment of that we had to push through, but a moment of like, we just spent nine months on this Conan O'Brien packet.
但我只记得，那就像是一个我们需要挺过去的时刻，但也是那种我们刚在这个柯南·奥布莱恩申请包上花了九个月时间的时刻。

00:24:44.259 --> 00:24:49.059
And you're telling us we have to generate a top 10 list and we only have two days to do it.
而你却告诉我们必须制作一个前十名榜单，而且我们只有两天时间。

00:24:49.059 --> 00:24:49.900
That's outrageous.
这太离谱了。

00:24:49.900 --> 00:24:55.539
And like, you know, the joke being to write a top 10 list, you have like three hours to do it when you get the job.
而且，你知道，开玩笑地说，写一个前十名榜单，等你得到这份工作时，你大概只有三个小时的时间来做。

00:24:55.539 --> 00:25:02.859
Yeah, but yeah, that was a very long weekend, but ultimately a successful one.
对，不过，那是一个非常漫长的周末，但最终是成功的。

00:25:03.579 --> 00:25:06.180
So you get the packet into them by Monday.
所以你们在周一之前把申请包交给了他们。

00:25:06.180 --> 00:25:07.299
When do you hear that you're hired?
你们什么时候听说被录用了？

00:25:08.059 --> 00:25:12.099
Like the next few days into that week, our lives change, our lives change, like what?
就像那周接下来的几天里，我们的生活变了，我们的生活变了，怎么变的？

00:25:12.099 --> 00:25:15.099
Like maybe the next week after that, we started a Carter.
就像也许在那之后的第二周，我们开始了一项工作。

00:25:15.099 --> 00:25:16.619
I was like, it was so fast.
我当时觉得，这一切太快了。

00:25:16.779 --> 00:25:20.380
Yeah, we had like a, we did one interview with Tim Long, who was the head writer.
对，我们当时就像是，我们和首席编剧蒂姆·朗进行了一次面试。

00:25:20.380 --> 00:25:59.910
And then shout out to Tim as well, shout out to Tim and Kate Adler who was the producer at the time and she she the two of them sort of bumped us up to the next and then the second interview was on the 12th floor Which I think we didn't we didn't understand the meaning of that at the time But in in the letterman days the 12th floor was Dave's floor like that's that's like the that's like the the main production floor of Letterman and we But it was with Rob Ernett, the executive producer at the time, and Rob, you know, we file in, we go up the elevator to this, and it's not a big, it's not like a labyrinth.
然后也要感谢蒂姆，感谢蒂姆和当时的制片人凯特·阿德勒，他们两个把我们推荐到了下一关，然后第二面试在12层，我想我们当时并不明白这意味着什么，但在莱特曼的时代，12层是戴夫的楼层，就像那是莱特曼主要的制作楼层，我们但是那是和当时的执行制片人罗伯·伯尼特进行的，罗伯，你知道的，我们鱼贯而入，我们坐电梯上去，这不是一个很大的，这不是什么迷宫。

00:25:59.910 --> 00:26:17.809
It's like an L-shape of the floor plan of the 12th floor, but we go in, and he's down at one end, and we go into Rob's office, and I remember Rob, this is how young we were, we had our resumes attached to the thing, and they were like, and Rob, I remember Rob being like, well, their grades are really good.
12楼的平面图呈L形，但我们进去了，他在一端，我们进了罗伯的办公室，我还记得罗伯，我们当时有多年轻啊，我们把简历附在资料上了，他们就像是，而且罗伯，我记得罗伯当时说，嗯，他们的成绩真的很好。

00:26:17.809 --> 00:26:19.809
We got a GPA.
我们有一个GPA。

00:26:19.809 --> 00:26:22.450
They're like, good GPAs.
他们就像是，绩点不错。

00:26:22.450 --> 00:26:25.609
But yeah, in that moment, he was like, this is on the month of October.
但是，在那个时刻，他说了，这是在十月份。

00:26:25.609 --> 00:26:26.609
Yeah.
对。

00:26:26.609 --> 00:26:27.609
Not bad.
还不错。

00:26:27.609 --> 00:26:28.609
I know CPR.
我会做心肺复苏。

00:26:28.609 --> 00:26:30.410
I don't know CPR.
我不会做心肺复苏。

00:26:30.410 --> 00:26:36.490
But, but that was on a Friday and he basically said, like, they seemed good to me.
但是，那是在一个周五，他基本上说过，他们在我看来挺合适的。

00:26:36.490 --> 00:26:39.049
Then he turned to us and it was like, enjoy this weekend.
然后他转向我们，就像是说，好好享受这个周末吧。

00:26:39.049 --> 00:26:42.849
This is as good as this job is going to get because starting Monday is going to be terrible.
这份工作也就这样了，因为从周一开始会糟糕透顶。

00:26:42.849 --> 00:26:45.450
But, you know, come in on Monday and you're hired.
不过，你知道的，周一来上班吧，你被录用了。

00:26:45.450 --> 00:26:57.829
And we walked out and we're so stunned that we got lost in the complex L shape floor plan of the toes and no way to get lost, but we got lost all the way down the hall.
然后我们走了出去，震惊得在那个L形楼层的复杂布局里迷了路，明明没道理会迷路，但我们一路迷到了走廊尽头。

00:26:57.829 --> 00:27:01.930
We turned the only corner, we turned the corner, the only corner of the whole hallway.
我们转过唯一的拐角，我们转过那个拐角，整条走廊唯一的拐角。

00:27:01.930 --> 00:27:06.609
And we turn the corner and like you can film this in your mind, it's it's high noon.
当我们转过拐角时——你可以把它想象成电影画面——当时正是正午。

00:27:06.609 --> 00:27:13.849
And there are the other end is David Letterman, like standing alone, like I think he was like tossing a football, just standing there.
在走廊的另一头是大卫·莱特曼，独自一人站在那儿，我觉得他当时好像在抛着橄榄球，就站在那儿。

00:27:13.849 --> 00:27:20.359
like he's maybe going to kick our ass or something, and we say to him, do you know where the elevator is?
就像他可能要揍我们一顿或者干嘛似的，然后我们对他说：“你知道电梯在哪里吗？”

00:27:20.359 --> 00:27:22.960
And he says, it doesn't fall.
他说：“它不会掉下去。”

00:27:22.960 --> 00:27:27.839
And like I love, I remember that particular phrasing, do you know where the elevator is?
我特别喜欢，我记得那个特定的说法，你居然问“你知道电梯在哪里吗”？

00:27:27.839 --> 00:27:30.960
Like this building that has your name on it in giant lights?
就好像这座楼上还挂着你巨型灯牌的名字？

00:27:30.960 --> 00:27:31.960
Wow.
哇。

00:27:31.960 --> 00:27:32.960
You do it the other way.
你总是反其道而行之。

00:27:32.960 --> 00:27:34.839
That's an incredibly charming story.
这真是一个极其迷人的故事。

00:27:34.839 --> 00:27:38.880
I love that you guys read in him and this mythic kind of character.
我喜欢你们把她和他看作那种神话般的人物。

00:27:38.880 --> 00:27:39.880
Yeah.
对。

00:27:39.880 --> 00:27:40.880
And I think we were wearing backpacks.
而且我觉得我们当时还背着双包。

00:27:40.880 --> 00:27:42.079
I think we brought backpacks in the meaning.
我觉得我们面试时背了背包。

00:27:42.079 --> 00:27:44.839
He called us the backpack boys behind our back for years.
他背地里叫了我们好几年“背包男孩”。

00:27:44.839 --> 00:27:55.599
Yeah, the backpack was our nickname, the worst boy, the worst boy man of all time, the, the rough draft is the most be boys and the most be boys, so you guys are there.
对，背包就是我们的外号，史上最逊的男孩，最逊的成年男孩，初稿就是最像B男孩的组合，所以你们就在那儿。

00:27:55.599 --> 00:27:56.599
How long?
多久？

00:27:56.599 --> 00:27:57.599
Six years.
六年。

00:27:57.599 --> 00:27:58.599
Four and a half years.
四年半。

00:27:58.599 --> 00:27:59.599
Oh, four and a half.
哦，四年半。

00:27:59.599 --> 00:28:00.599
Four and a half.
四年半。

00:28:00.599 --> 00:28:01.599
Okay.
好吧。

00:28:01.599 --> 00:28:07.440
The next time we talk today, after that story was just when we said goodbye, we probably talked about three other times in between.
今天我们聊到这个故事之后，直到刚刚说再见前，我们中间大概又聊了其他三次。

00:28:07.440 --> 00:28:15.680
Tell me, what did you guys learn at Letterman that helped you write and run how I met your mother?
告诉我，你们在莱特曼那里学到了什么，对你们编写和运作《老爸老妈浪漫史》有什么帮助？

00:28:15.680 --> 00:28:16.920
Everything, right, Carter?
所有东西，对吧，卡特？

00:28:16.920 --> 00:28:18.920
Like, everything.
就像，所有东西。

00:28:18.920 --> 00:28:26.240
After Letterman, we were on a couple of other sitcoms that are sort of forgettable, didn't really go the distance.
在莱特曼之后，我们参与过其他几部情景喜剧，都挺容易被遗忘的，也没能长久。

00:28:26.240 --> 00:28:28.960
Although we're an American dad, which is still on TV, which is amazing.
虽然我们参与了《美国老爹》，现在还在电视上播，这太神奇了。

00:28:28.960 --> 00:28:30.359
We're in there for one season.
我们在那儿待了一季。

00:28:30.359 --> 00:28:34.720
But basically, everything we needed to learn to be a showrunner, we learned at Letterman.
但基本上，作为一个剧集运作人需要学的一切，我们都在莱特曼那里学到了。

00:28:34.720 --> 00:28:43.059
And then when you go on to a sitcom staff, a lot of the ethos at least back in the day when we were getting into it was like the writers weren't in the edit room.
然后当你加入情景喜剧剧组时，许多理念——至少在我们刚入行时——就是编剧们不用进剪辑室。

00:28:43.059 --> 00:28:44.740
The writers weren't necessarily always on set.
编剧们不一定总是在片场。

00:28:44.740 --> 00:28:48.900
The writers sort of wrote things and were that part of the assembly line.
编剧们基本上就是写写东西，成为那条流水线的一部分。

00:28:48.900 --> 00:28:54.160
On Letterman, you had, if you had an idea in the morning, you had to produce it by the showtime.
而在莱特曼，如果你早上有个点子，你必须在节目播出前把它制作出来。

00:28:54.160 --> 00:28:55.160
You had to get your music.
你得选好音乐。

00:28:55.160 --> 00:28:56.160
You had to order up your graphics.
你得订购你的图表。

00:28:56.160 --> 00:28:59.400
You had to, if there was like Kiron and like titles involved, you had every piece of it.
你得，如果有什么字幕和片头之类的话，每一个部分你都要参与。

00:28:59.400 --> 00:29:00.400
You went on the shoot.
你要去拍摄现场。

00:29:00.400 --> 00:29:02.720
You basically kind of directed or co-directed your bit.
你基本上要执导或联合执导你的短片。

00:29:02.720 --> 00:29:14.359
you had with the director and make sure you got everything you needed, get into an edit room, book your time there, get with the editor, like every part of it was you, every part of it you were responsible for producing.
你和导演合作，确保得到你需要的一切，进入剪辑室，预定那里的时间，和剪辑师沟通，每一个环节都是你，每一个环节你都负责制作。

00:29:14.359 --> 00:29:28.200
And that was such good training for being a showrunner because by the time, I think by the time some writers who've only ever been on sitcom staffs, they become a showrunner, they've never really spent a lot of time in edit, they've never done a sound mix, they've never, we had done all that stuff.
这对于成为一个剧集运作人是极好的训练，因为相比之下，有些只待过情景喜剧剧组的编剧在成为剧集运作人时，从来没有真正在剪辑室待过很长时间，从来没有做过混音，从来没有……而这些我们全都做过。

00:29:28.200 --> 00:29:35.920
It can't be overstated how much like technology and like the editing software that exists in a like eye movie coming built into your laptop.
这绝对不能被夸大，比如现代科技以及笔记本电脑里自带的像iMovie这样的剪辑软件。

00:29:35.920 --> 00:29:44.480
How much that has changed the world because at the time it really was like us knowing how to go into an edit room was like like we'd gone to Hogwarts.
这改变了多少世界，因为当时我们懂得怎么进剪辑室，简直就像去了霍格沃茨一样。

00:29:44.480 --> 00:29:56.920
Like we like had this like mystical power that like like really like no one understood how to like you know trim 15 frames off of something like like dumb simple stuff that anyone in the world now knows.
就像我们拥有某种神秘的力量，真的，没人懂怎么去——你知道的，从某个画面里剪掉15帧——这种现在世界上任何人都会的愚蠢简单的事情。

00:29:56.920 --> 00:30:04.240
It was really that letterman definitely gave us a chance to basically go to film school for four years.
真的，莱特曼绝对给了我们一个机会，基本上相当于上了四年电影学院。

00:30:04.240 --> 00:30:07.359
It was film school and comedy school in a big way.
在很大程度上，它既是电影学院，也是喜剧学校。

00:30:07.359 --> 00:30:21.640
I also feel like the whole 10,000 hours thing of learning how to, I definitely feel like I learned how to write a joke and how to write something that gets a laugh.
我也觉得关于学习写东西的整个“一万小时定律”嘛，我绝对觉得自己学会了怎么写笑话，怎么写能逗笑别人的东西。

00:30:21.640 --> 00:30:31.119
I don't even think that myself is necessarily a funny person, but I knew what the mechanics were of had, and the only way to learn that is doing it over and over and failing a lot.
我甚至不觉得自己本身是个多搞笑的人，但我知道其中的机制是什么，而学习这个的唯一方法就是一遍又一遍地去做，经历大量的失败。

00:30:31.119 --> 00:30:34.300
And like, yeah, I wanted to ask you about the failure.
而且，呃，对，我想问问你关于失败的事。

00:30:34.300 --> 00:30:37.245
Did it, it did it, thicken your skin considerably.
它，它有没有让你的脸皮厚了很多？

00:30:37.245 --> 00:30:47.966
So you, you pitch jokes, obviously, to these older, more veteran writers, and they're swatting them down or ideas are getting, you know, batted down or something doesn't get a laugh.
所以你，你显然会向这些资历更老、更有经验的编剧抛出笑话，然后他们会否定，或者各种点子被，你知道的，直接枪毙，或者某个点子根本没逗笑大家。

00:30:47.966 --> 00:30:53.680
Like, did you feel like that, which didn't kill you made you stronger kind of by the end of that?
就，你有没有觉得那种“杀不死你的终将使你更强大”的感觉，在那段经历结束时油然而生？

00:30:53.680 --> 00:30:54.548
Yeah, definitely.
对，绝对有。

00:30:54.548 --> 00:30:57.920
And it was very humbling and it told you it was a volume business.
而且这非常磨练人，它让你明白这是一个靠产出数量的行业。

00:30:57.920 --> 00:30:59.342
You needed to have an extra joke.
你需要准备备用笑话。

00:30:59.342 --> 00:31:00.764
You needed to have an extra idea.
你需要准备备用点子。

00:31:00.764 --> 00:31:05.980
There was no, there's no writers block when you're doing five shows a week and you're getting paid decent money to do it.
当你一周要做五个节目，而且拿了还不错的薪水时，根本没有，根本没有什么“创作瓶颈”。

00:31:05.980 --> 00:31:06.756
There's no writer.
根本没有这种事。

00:31:06.756 --> 00:31:07.833
You have to have an idea.
你必须得有灵感。

00:31:07.833 --> 00:31:08.911
You have to have an idea.
你必须得有东西可写。

00:31:08.911 --> 00:31:13.997
And even if it's not great, you have to do, you have to produce it and make it and hope that it gets on and does okay.
哪怕它不是绝佳的，你也必须得做，你必须把它创作并制作出来，然后祈祷它能播出口碑还不错。

00:31:13.997 --> 00:31:17.920
Carter and I did the viewer mail segment for four years of our four and a half years there.
我和卡特在那待了四年半，其中有四年都在负责观众来信环节。

00:31:17.920 --> 00:31:21.651
And you, we would go down for the taping of that segment and be in the audience.
然后，我们会去录制那个环节，坐在观众席里。

00:31:21.651 --> 00:31:33.920
And you had to sit in that audience and we stood up to the side and we would sit there and listen to those played and either get a laugh from the audience or not get a laugh from the audience and what didn't get a laugh and it was dead silent and Dave's up there.
你必须坐在那个观众席里，我们就站在一旁，坐在那儿听着那些内容被播放出来，要么逗笑观众，要么没逗笑观众；而那些没逗笑大家的、现场一片死寂的时候，戴夫（Dave）就在上面。

00:31:33.920 --> 00:31:39.160
This iconic, wonderful, I mean, I idolized Dave, I love him, I made that joke before, but I love Dave.
那个标志性的、了不起的——我是说，我把戴夫奉为偶像，我爱他，我之前开过玩笑了，但我真的很爱戴夫。

00:31:39.160 --> 00:31:42.440
When he would turn on it, he'd go like, oh, that was a tough one, Paul.
每当情况不妙时，他就会说，哦，这期有点难办啊，保罗。

00:31:42.440 --> 00:31:49.279
I feel like we're getting torn a new one on national television by our hero or at least my hero.
我觉得我们正被我们的英雄——或者至少是我的英雄——在全国电视观众面前扒掉一层皮。

00:31:49.279 --> 00:31:51.013
And there's nothing more humbling.
没有什么比这更磨练人的了。

00:31:51.013 --> 00:31:53.920
There's no thicker skin to be developed than that moment.
没有哪个时刻比那更能让人练就厚脸皮了。

00:31:53.920 --> 00:31:58.720
And you got to scrape yourself off the floor and say, I got at least one more shot at this next week if we don't get fired.
你只能从地上爬起来，对自己说：如果我们没被炒鱿鱼的话，下周我至少还有一次机会。

00:31:59.200 --> 00:32:00.983
And you just work even harder.
然后你就会更加努力地工作。

00:32:00.983 --> 00:32:03.599
And you develop that ability to bounce back.
你也就培养出了那种迅速振作起来的能力。

